1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,040
Welcome to Launched.

2
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:19,120
I'm Charlie Chapman, and today I'm really excited to be joined by the indie app developer behind Drag Thing, Peacock, and Dice, as well as seasoned podcast veteran, guest on all kinds of shows with The Incomparable and Real AFM, and probably way more than I know about.

3
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:21,600
Welcome to the show, James Thompson.

4
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:35,600
Thanks for coming.

5
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:36,720
Thanks for having me.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,560
It's kind of it's nice to branch out into somewhere new as well.

7
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:42,960
Yeah.

8
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:46,960
Yeah, I'm probably a person whose name you actually don't know already.

9
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:50,640
Well, I mean, I've got dark noise on my phone, so.

10
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:51,200
Really?

11
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:51,600
Yeah.

12
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:52,320
Oh man.

13
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:53,680
Well, that's cool to hear.

14
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:54,800
No, I really like it.

15
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:57,520
I was kind of playing around with it again just there.

16
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:00,480
And it's like, yeah, this is really nice.

17
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:01,200
Awesome.

18
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:02,080
Well, thank you.

19
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:12,640
So I want to start all these out with an icebreaker question just to kind of keep the conversational tone since no one really knows who I am anyway, so I kind of want to have that conversational, you know.

20
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:17,920
So eventually I want those to be coming from listeners.

21
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:22,640
And I actually do have a couple, but for you, I have a particular icebreaker question that I wanted to ask.

22
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:28,160
You're a frequent guest on the Incomparable, so you're kind of a media guru, at least from my perspective.

23
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:33,280
So my question is, what is your preference for how to watch TV shows?

24
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:40,880
Do you prefer like the weekly drop that's been sort of coming with Apple TV and I guess not Disney Plus for you yet over in Scotland?

25
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:44,240
Or do you prefer the sort of Netflix style drop everything at once?

26
00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:51,760
I would have said I like to drop everything at once.

27
00:01:52,560 --> 00:02:00,640
But, you know, there's been with Apple TV, there's a number of shows like For All Mankind and Sea and stuff, which I'm actually really enjoying.

28
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:08,640
And it's kinda nice not to get it all at once, you know, because people actually can talk about episodes.

29
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:13,760
And, you know, there's other shows, you know, things like The Mandalorian or whatever.

30
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:37,760
And it seems people can talk about them on a weekly basis and there's sort of space to talk about what just happened, rather than, oh, here's twenty episodes of something and then some people will burn through it in a couple of days and some people will take weeks and there's not the same kind of, you know, water cooler, virtual water cooler talk about it.

31
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:42,800
So I think it actually is benefiting these shows to drop them once a week.

32
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:46,160
Yeah, I definitely think it's like it depends on the show kind of thing probably.

33
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:52,480
Yeah, I mean for things that I don't actually care about, you know, if they're all drops at once, that's fine.

34
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:57,360
I mean, don't get me wrong, I would like to see the next episodes right, right.

35
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:03,600
Instantly of these things, but, you know, I think it'ss nice to have a certain amount of anticipation.

36
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:04,400
Yeah.

37
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:07,760
It's interesting too that they're all like dropping on Fridays, it seems like.

38
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:08,080
Yeah.

39
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:16,240
So you kind of come back from the week and you got all these shows to find all your friends at work or on your virtual work, I guess, um, to talk to you about.

40
00:03:16,640 --> 00:03:16,800
Yeah.

41
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:19,920
And I, you know, I'd like them to space it out a bit.

42
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:24,400
I mean, at least Doctor Who's going to be Sunday, I think, for the next series.

43
00:03:24,880 --> 00:03:28,160
So that's not all happening on, on Friday, Saturday or whatever.

44
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:28,800
But yeah.

45
00:03:29,280 --> 00:03:32,560
Um, there's just a bit too many shows at the moment.

46
00:03:32,720 --> 00:03:35,280
I know it's I shouldn't complain about that.

47
00:03:35,920 --> 00:03:40,800
But there's like, you know, Watchmen is really worth watching.

48
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:47,520
And all these things, it's just, it's either there's too many or there's not enough.

49
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:49,680
And we just space out.

50
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:50,080
Yeah.

51
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:59,200
And everyone like fighting to win this streaming war thing at the same time probably means we're going to get the highest amount of high quality content right now.

52
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:03,280
And then it's all just going to drop out from underneath us once someone wins.

53
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:04,000
Yeah.

54
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:12,480
I mean, I think we're all spending the money to see these thirteen streaming networks to get to see.

55
00:04:13,840 --> 00:04:16,320
And I don't think that's going to be sustainable in the long run.

56
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:21,200
But I think that's true of subscriptions in general, but that's a whole other conversation.

57
00:04:22,080 --> 00:04:22,640
Yeah, yeah.

58
00:04:22,960 --> 00:04:28,800
We we probably won't touch on that, but that would be one that I'd be interested in hearing your thoughts on anyway some other time.

59
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:40,000
So before we get into like your actual apps, I kind of want to get like a quick rundown for people who don't already know who you are, which is probably almost nobody at this point.

60
00:04:40,240 --> 00:04:43,520
But, you know, so I'd like to know, where are you from?

61
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,800
How did you learn, like formal education or self-taught?

62
00:04:47,040 --> 00:04:50,880
And then what was your career, let's say pre-drag thing in this case.

63
00:04:51,680 --> 00:04:55,600
So, um, yeah, I'm from Glasgow in Scotland.

64
00:04:57,040 --> 00:05:09,600
My accent sounds a bit in the middle between Scotland and England because my mum grew up in the south of England, so I kind of like sound English in Scotland and Scotland in England.

65
00:05:10,160 --> 00:05:12,960
Yeah, it's, uh, it's confusing.

66
00:05:13,200 --> 00:06:02,880
But anyway, yes, I started out, I mean really to go all the way back, uh, I got a Commodore 64 in, I think around 1983 and around about the same time as that my school and this was in primary seven we got a BBC model B computer which is a particularly UK thing but it had a really good basic programming language on it and you could do graphics and various other things with it so when I got those machines or I got access to those machines and the I started out because I'mm like ten at this point.

67
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,720
I started out playing games on them because that's what you do.

68
00:06:06,960 --> 00:06:07,120
Of course.

69
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:27,120
And there was one series of games in particular from a developer called Jeff Mentor who he started out, you know, in the early 80s and there were Commodore 64 games with titles such as Revenge of the Mutant Camels, which was the sequel, of course, to Attack of the Mutant Camels.

70
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:35,360
And he did all these games and they kind of all involved some kind of sheep, camel, llama things.

71
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:51,040
And he taught me that a single person could make something because he always put his name on it, like the title screen, and that you could put a certain amount of your personality into your software.

72
00:06:51,760 --> 00:06:53,600
So I wanted to make games like him.

73
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:57,200
I met him once at a computer show down in London.

74
00:06:59,040 --> 00:07:02,880
And so that was the sort of like, well, how do you do this?

75
00:07:03,680 --> 00:07:08,320
And I started messing around with Commodore 64 and with the BBC Basic.

76
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:12,560
And I knew at that point that I wanted to go on to.

77
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:18,880
to go on to do computing as whatever my career was going to be.

78
00:07:19,440 --> 00:07:27,840
So I ended up doing a degree in computer science at Glasgow University, about five minutes walk away from where I'm currently living.

79
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:35,360
And there I first encountered the Macintosh.

80
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:39,600
And at this point it was sort of SEs and that era of machine.

81
00:07:39,920 --> 00:07:41,680
This is sort of this is 1990.

82
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:42,080
Okay.

83
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:47,040
And it was like the Mac was just so much better than anything else I'd used.

84
00:07:47,360 --> 00:07:48,800
I had an Atari ST at this point.

85
00:07:49,680 --> 00:07:58,480
And I realized that the user interface of the Mac had been the inspiration for the ST's user interface and they had ripped it off poorly.

86
00:07:59,840 --> 00:08:10,400
So it was like seeing this and seeing the kind of how well thought through everything was, how well designed it was, it was like, well, you know, I've got to make apps for this thing.

87
00:08:10,560 --> 00:08:13,360
Well, they were called applications back then, but yeah.

88
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:27,760
And so my app that I wrote to learn how to program the Mac, the first thing I ever wrote starting around 91, 92 was indeed PCalc because I wanted an app.

89
00:08:28,560 --> 00:08:39,680
I wanted a scientific calculator app and I wanted one that did, you know, hex and octave and binary and that because I was using that in my course and I hadn't found a decent one for the Mac and I thought, well, this is a small enough project I can do it.

90
00:08:40,320 --> 00:09:00,160
The other way that this came from was one of the classes that I was doing was user interface design and one of the projects that we had was to design a central heating control panel and this was done in Hypercard at this point.

91
00:09:00,880 --> 00:09:14,800
So I did this and as part of it I had created this fake seven segment LCD display and I had all these buttons and I was like, well, I can reuse all these assets that I've made for a calculator.

92
00:09:15,680 --> 00:09:21,680
And that was basically the decision as to why I make a calculator was I had already drawn these graphics.

93
00:09:22,960 --> 00:09:27,440
So I wrote PCalc and I released it as freeware in 1992.

94
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:34,480
And I went on and I wrote a drag thing in 1994, 1995.

95
00:09:36,960 --> 00:09:45,120
All this was just learning new things, like DragThing was learning new drag and drop APIs that had appeared in System 7.5, I think it was.

96
00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:59,920
And all this stuff was sort of learning, but in the release notes for all these things, it basically said, I wanted to work for Apple, you know, so if there's anyone out there at Apple who can get me a job, please do.

97
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:02,960
You were using that as like an easter egg resume?

98
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:12,560
Yeah, it was putting it out into the world and at this point the stuff was free and it was like, let's see if it can be popular and if this can get me a job at Apple.

99
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:16,080
That one question I did have is how were you distributing that?

100
00:10:16,400 --> 00:10:17,120
Was that over the internet?

101
00:10:18,080 --> 00:10:22,080
Yeah, I mean, this was certainly with PCalk, it was pre web.

102
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:22,720
Right.

103
00:10:23,040 --> 00:10:28,800
So like the web for me, kind of I was first aware of it sort of like 94, 95.

104
00:10:28,960 --> 00:10:48,720
So this was 92 and there was a thing called the InfoMac archive and it was an FTP archive of software and you could submit your software to this and there was a Usenet group that kind of you could post stuff to and it would auto submit and so on.

105
00:10:48,720 --> 00:10:54,320
And then there would be a roundup each day, I think it was, of the new things that had gone up on this.

106
00:10:54,560 --> 00:10:56,000
So you could Interesting.

107
00:10:56,480 --> 00:11:02,320
So you could subscribe to this and you could get like, oh, here's the new software for the for the week or whatever.

108
00:11:02,560 --> 00:11:02,720
Right.

109
00:11:02,960 --> 00:11:12,240
And yeah, it was all pre, pre web and, you know, it was kind of interesting to do all this stuff before that point, you know?

110
00:11:12,480 --> 00:11:20,000
And I mean, there was like, well, it was literally, it would go on floppy disks on the front of magazines and that, and that sort of thing.

111
00:11:20,560 --> 00:11:30,000
And there was like shareware library type places where you could write off and get a paper catalog and then send off for things and you get them back on disk.

112
00:11:30,800 --> 00:11:39,040
Yeah, because like you said it was, it was freeware, but at that point, unless you were going to like work with a retailer or something, was there really a way to sell it?

113
00:11:39,920 --> 00:11:42,240
I mean, those things were starting at that point.

114
00:11:42,640 --> 00:11:50,720
I mean, I'm trying to think, I don't know, can't remember what year something like Doom was, which, you know, was distributed to shareware.

115
00:11:52,160 --> 00:12:01,840
But it was difficult, but there were companies like Caggy, who are the people I ended up eventually using for selling pcalc and drag thing at first anyway.

116
00:12:02,560 --> 00:12:09,680
They probably started around, I'm guessing around 95, 96, that time frame.

117
00:12:11,120 --> 00:12:26,080
What my software was, I mean, besides being a sort of calling card and trying to get me a job because I was at university and I wanted a job at the end of this, it was also I had some of them as kind of coolware or postcardware or something.

118
00:12:26,480 --> 00:12:30,240
So people would send me just random postcards and things from around the world.

119
00:12:30,560 --> 00:12:35,280
I mean, this was like the day I put my home address in the about box of this software.

120
00:12:35,520 --> 00:12:36,800
Yeah, I can't imagine doing that now.

121
00:12:37,680 --> 00:12:40,160
No, I absolutely wouldn't do that now.

122
00:12:40,560 --> 00:12:47,600
Although I also can't imagine downloading random applications to my computer that have like full system access and just running them.

123
00:12:48,480 --> 00:12:50,880
Yeah, I mean, it was more innocent times.

124
00:12:51,200 --> 00:12:51,280
Yeah.

125
00:12:53,120 --> 00:13:12,960
In many ways, but by 1994 I graduated and I ended up actually getting a job at the university where I had been a bachelor and I was working on the systems team who were the people who, you know, I was basically a system administrator, watching for the systems.

126
00:13:12,960 --> 00:13:16,080
or looking after the same labs of max that I had used before.

127
00:13:16,640 --> 00:13:24,960
It was basically because I had complained because I was a student rep, and I would be complaining to the system staff constantly about the state of all the systems.

128
00:13:25,360 --> 00:13:29,760
So my punishment for this was I was put in charge of them and now you have to fix it.

129
00:13:30,080 --> 00:13:33,440
And then I had undergraduates complaining to me about the state of the stuff.

130
00:13:33,600 --> 00:13:36,000
So, you know, it was like a circle of life and all that.

131
00:13:36,880 --> 00:13:48,000
And while I was working there, you know, I was writing some in house software for the department, learning more and more about writing Mac apps.

132
00:13:48,800 --> 00:13:50,480
So it was a good sort of training round.

133
00:13:51,280 --> 00:14:02,160
And then at some point in this, I think it was late 1996, I got an email from someone at Apple who said, Oh, I saw your read me in drag thing.

134
00:14:03,040 --> 00:14:04,000
Do you want a job?

135
00:14:04,960 --> 00:14:06,160
And I was like, Yeah.

136
00:14:07,120 --> 00:14:11,520
So I this was I went over for an interview.

137
00:14:11,840 --> 00:14:13,360
I mean, it wasn't just a job offer.

138
00:14:13,680 --> 00:14:15,840
It was an offer of an interview at least.

139
00:14:15,920 --> 00:14:16,160
Right.

140
00:14:16,560 --> 00:14:17,440
To come to California.

141
00:14:17,840 --> 00:14:18,720
Well, this was the thing.

142
00:14:18,880 --> 00:14:19,760
It wasn't California.

143
00:14:20,160 --> 00:14:21,280
This was Cork in Ireland.

144
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:22,880
So interesting.

145
00:14:25,600 --> 00:14:31,200
was close by, you know, it was it was still not Scotland, but it, you know, it was just a short hop across the water.

146
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:39,840
And in Cork they had what was primarily a manufacturing plant, which had been making things like from the Apple 2 days.

147
00:14:41,120 --> 00:14:45,760
There was a point I think they were like fabricating their own silicon in the factory or something.

148
00:14:46,080 --> 00:14:49,280
It was like, you know, sand goes in one end and computers come out the other.

149
00:14:49,600 --> 00:14:49,680
Wow.

150
00:14:50,320 --> 00:14:58,000
But at this point they also had software teams to support some of the hardware and some of the hardware was designed there as well.

151
00:14:58,240 --> 00:15:00,640
Like there was a TV tuner car.

152
00:15:00,640 --> 00:15:09,200
Tuner card that was on some of the you could get for some of the early Macintoshes and the software for that was written in Cork and I think the hardware was developed there too.

153
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:20,960
And at that point when I went over, they had a pretty big staff because I think there was about 150 engineers working on stuff.

154
00:15:21,840 --> 00:15:34,320
And this was for macOS 8, the original macOS 8, which was called Copeland, which was an operating system that Apple was writing to replace classic macOS.

155
00:15:35,280 --> 00:15:40,880
So memory protected all the kind of modern things, but it's not macOS 10.

156
00:15:41,920 --> 00:15:47,280
And Apple had been saying for a number of years at WWDC, this is the future, you know.

157
00:15:47,520 --> 00:15:49,440
Yeah, this was the one that was always coming, right?

158
00:15:49,920 --> 00:15:50,000
Yeah.

159
00:15:51,520 --> 00:15:58,960
And so I went over, had the interview, was offered a job and I moved over and my wife moved over with me.

160
00:15:59,920 --> 00:16:03,600
Not my wife then actually, but I'll come to that later.

161
00:16:04,480 --> 00:16:08,560
And I joined and they canceled the project.

162
00:16:09,040 --> 00:16:17,200
I was on the Copeland installer team for two weeks and the whole operating system was cancelled.

163
00:16:17,520 --> 00:16:18,400
What year was this again?

164
00:16:19,120 --> 00:16:21,360
This would be, I think, late 1996.

165
00:16:21,840 --> 00:16:21,920
Okay.

166
00:16:22,320 --> 00:16:27,200
So there was kind of a disbelief that the thing had been cancelled because people had been working on this for years.

167
00:16:27,840 --> 00:16:30,320
And they'd been talking publicly about it for a long time, right?

168
00:16:30,800 --> 00:16:42,560
Yeah, I think there had been, I think, at least two WWDCs where, you know, this was coming and there had been early alpha builds of the OS sent to developers and things.

169
00:16:42,800 --> 00:16:46,400
You know, this was, this was like serious investment by Apple.

170
00:16:47,040 --> 00:16:48,640
Slightly bigger deal than AirPower.

171
00:16:49,680 --> 00:16:50,640
Oh yeah, yeah, no.

172
00:16:51,840 --> 00:16:59,280
This was I dread to think how many hundreds of millions of dollars and people's time went into this.

173
00:17:00,080 --> 00:17:06,800
And there were people still working on stuff when I got there, even though it had been cancelled, because nobody actually believed that it would really be cancelled.

174
00:17:07,120 --> 00:17:09,920
It's like when a war ends before the phone.

175
00:17:10,320 --> 00:17:10,960
Yeah, exactly.

176
00:17:11,120 --> 00:17:19,680
So there were these developers hiding out in the jungle, unaware that they say to this day there's still one developer in a corner in Cork.

177
00:17:20,240 --> 00:17:20,320
Yes.

178
00:17:20,880 --> 00:17:24,000
And when I went, it was like clear.

179
00:17:24,720 --> 00:17:31,120
And it hadn't been clear to me on the outside, and I should have clearly not done my research that Apple was in a bad way at this point.

180
00:17:31,680 --> 00:17:44,640
You know, they were like my manager resigned shortly after I joined, and there was like, Apple was, you know, one quarter away from bankruptcy at this point.

181
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,320
It was not long for this world.

182
00:17:49,280 --> 00:18:00,400
And they brought in Gil Emilio, who was this hatchet man, who was basically brought in to Axe a lot of jobs and lots of people.

183
00:18:00,960 --> 00:18:06,800
I know were fired and whenever people would leave, they wouldn't be replaced.

184
00:18:07,280 --> 00:18:15,440
And, you know, the entire engineering team by the end of when I was at Apple, we all fitted around one table at lunchtime.

185
00:18:15,760 --> 00:18:31,280
It was, man, it gone from a lot of people and a lot of test organization because they, one of Apple's things at the time was they wanted testers in, uh, US time zone, uh, in Japan and in Europe.

186
00:18:31,840 --> 00:18:34,160
So they could basically have 24 hour testing.

187
00:18:34,560 --> 00:18:35,920
So there would be a build come out.

188
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:41,520
And by the time the engineers got back in the next morning, you know, it had gone through a full cycle of testing.

189
00:18:41,840 --> 00:18:42,480
That was the theory.

190
00:18:42,720 --> 00:18:43,280
Oh yeah.

191
00:18:44,320 --> 00:18:47,120
So that was, I mean, that was one of the reasons why they had all the stuff.

192
00:18:47,360 --> 00:18:57,120
But because, you know, Apple was running out of money, they were reducing all the external stuff and bringing stuff back into Cupertino as much as they could.

193
00:18:59,040 --> 00:19:03,760
But, you know, despite all this, I got to work on some interesting stuff.

194
00:19:04,720 --> 00:19:10,640
I mean, the first thing that I did, or the first two projects I worked on in Apple, I've told this story before.

195
00:19:10,800 --> 00:19:12,640
So if anyone has heard it before, I'm sorry.

196
00:19:13,920 --> 00:19:25,680
But it was we were working for Disney because Apple was going to launch these consumer machines and they wanted some consumer grade software to include on them.

197
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:34,000
And Disney had all these print studios at the time, which are things where you could print out sort of greetings cards and envelopes and stickers and whatever.

198
00:19:34,240 --> 00:19:34,640
Oh, okay.

199
00:19:34,800 --> 00:19:35,200
Gotcha.

200
00:19:35,760 --> 00:19:38,880
And they had these for the PC and they wanted a version for the Mac.

201
00:19:39,200 --> 00:19:42,160
And the first one was 101 Dalmatians.

202
00:19:43,760 --> 00:19:55,040
And so Apple went to Disney and said, We want you to port this stuff to the Mac and, you know, we'll give you a million dollars or whatever it was.

203
00:19:55,600 --> 00:20:02,000
And Disney turned around to Apple and Cork and said, We'd like you to port this stuff and we'll give you like 500,000 dollars.

204
00:20:03,440 --> 00:20:08,240
And so we ended up working for Disney working for Apple, which was a really weird situation.

205
00:20:08,400 --> 00:20:08,480
Yeah.

206
00:20:08,720 --> 00:20:13,360
Because we had these two bosses and we were we would submit builds to Disney.

207
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,800
Um, but, you know, we were working for Apple.

208
00:20:17,600 --> 00:20:23,920
Uh, and so I did that and that shipped and that shipped with the performers at the time.

209
00:20:24,480 --> 00:20:27,680
And then we did a version based on the Hercules movie.

210
00:20:29,280 --> 00:20:36,160
And I mean, these were kind of, you know, sort of, I wouldn't say simple because nothing that involves printing is simple.

211
00:20:36,560 --> 00:20:36,720
Sure.

212
00:20:37,840 --> 00:20:47,360
But, you know, they were fairly straightforward applications and we got to we were taking the design of the Windows apps and rewriting it from scratch under the hood.

213
00:20:47,600 --> 00:20:51,360
So they looked the same and they felt the same, but it was all different code underneath.

214
00:20:51,760 --> 00:20:51,840
Yeah.

215
00:20:52,160 --> 00:20:57,680
I I was a Windows phone developer who was basically doing the exact same thing for a couple of years.

216
00:20:58,320 --> 00:21:05,520
Microsoft would give a bunch of money to a company to port their Android or iOS app over because nobody wanted it.

217
00:21:05,920 --> 00:21:07,600
And then that company didn't care at all.

218
00:21:07,760 --> 00:21:13,120
They would just turn around and hand the money to us and say, you have exactly this much money to port this thing over.

219
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:15,760
So I'm very familiar with that concept.

220
00:21:16,320 --> 00:21:16,480
Yeah.

221
00:21:16,800 --> 00:21:30,000
So, I mean, but it was an interesting work and I learned a lot of stuff about, you know, again, like we were doing this in C plus plus and using the Networks Code Warrior power plant framework for that stuff.

222
00:21:31,840 --> 00:21:38,000
But I wanted to work on like macOS 10 was kind of starting up internally at this time.

223
00:21:38,160 --> 00:21:39,520
And, you know, we were aware of it.

224
00:21:40,800 --> 00:21:45,360
And that was, the Apple didn't have quite the secrecy that it had later on.

225
00:21:46,640 --> 00:21:48,400
So, you know, we knew the stuff was there.

226
00:21:48,800 --> 00:21:51,520
And I wanted, I wanted to be involved in this.

227
00:21:52,160 --> 00:21:55,920
And I was like, my main thing was I like doing user interface design.

228
00:21:56,880 --> 00:22:07,600
And so they gave me a project working on a server authentication thing, which had no user interface whatsoever.

229
00:22:07,680 --> 00:22:07,760
Yeah.

230
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:08,480
The opposite.

231
00:22:08,880 --> 00:22:12,800
So it was, you know, all networking code and, you know, multithreaded.

232
00:22:13,360 --> 00:22:16,000
networking authentication, whatever.

233
00:22:16,560 --> 00:22:19,600
I was like the worst person to do on that project.

234
00:22:20,480 --> 00:22:26,480
And I worked on that for probably six months to a year or something.

235
00:22:28,320 --> 00:22:29,200
Maybe not that long.

236
00:22:29,840 --> 00:22:37,920
And it turned out that it was actually for the iMac, because at that point the iMac was going to be a discless workstation that net booted.

237
00:22:38,240 --> 00:22:38,560
Oh man.

238
00:22:38,800 --> 00:22:39,920
So it was called the iMac.

239
00:22:40,240 --> 00:22:43,280
This was pre like Jobs and Ives sort of thing.

240
00:22:43,600 --> 00:22:44,240
Well, no, no.

241
00:22:44,400 --> 00:22:45,680
Jobs had come back at this point.

242
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:46,160
Okay.

243
00:22:46,640 --> 00:22:52,160
But so this was 197, 98.

244
00:22:52,480 --> 00:22:52,560
Okay.

245
00:22:53,040 --> 00:22:54,240
That kind of time frame.

246
00:22:54,880 --> 00:22:59,040
But I think the project was still developing internally as to what it was going to be.

247
00:22:59,200 --> 00:23:04,320
And in the end, all that discless workstation stuff and my code along with it was thrown out.

248
00:23:05,200 --> 00:23:08,800
So, well, there's a pattern of that for my work at Apple.

249
00:23:10,720 --> 00:23:21,840
But one of the other things at the time was when I joined Apple, I said, I also got these apps that I do in my, you know, in my own time, Peacock and Drag thing.

250
00:23:22,400 --> 00:23:24,640
And I'd like to keep working on them in the evenings.

251
00:23:25,120 --> 00:23:30,240
and weekends and make that a condition of my employment.

252
00:23:31,120 --> 00:23:35,040
And Apple, well, Apple Cork at least said, Yeah, sure, no problem.

253
00:23:35,600 --> 00:23:38,240
And you absolutely wouldn't get a deal like that with Apple today.

254
00:23:38,880 --> 00:23:39,120
No way.

255
00:23:39,840 --> 00:23:45,280
You know, you see all these engineers who have got jobs at Apple and then just like their apps just disappear.

256
00:23:45,600 --> 00:23:45,680
Yeah.

257
00:23:46,960 --> 00:23:49,360
And it's always very sad when that happens.

258
00:23:49,680 --> 00:23:58,560
You know, you're happy for someone who's got a job and, you know, that probably their dream job is working for Apple, but, you know, their software is never going to get touched again.

259
00:23:59,040 --> 00:23:59,040
Yeah.

260
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:03,840
But I could work on these in my own time.

261
00:24:04,160 --> 00:24:09,760
And so, you know, I was keeping PCALT going and DragThing as well.

262
00:24:10,160 --> 00:24:21,200
And I had built into DragThing a timeout in the versions because I was fed up with people who would come to me and say, Oh, I found this bug.

263
00:24:21,360 --> 00:24:24,720
And I was like, Yeah, I fixed that in version 1.5.

264
00:24:26,080 --> 00:24:28,720
So they always, like, each build only lasted a year.

265
00:24:30,080 --> 00:24:36,640
And because I was working at Apple and I didn't have that much time to work on my own stuff, it was getting.

266
00:24:37,360 --> 00:24:38,640
very close to that year point.

267
00:24:39,360 --> 00:24:41,760
And because the app was free, you know, it didn't matter.

268
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:42,080
Yeah.

269
00:24:43,520 --> 00:24:49,600
And I think it was around that time that drag thing was coolware.

270
00:24:49,840 --> 00:24:51,200
You know, I said, send me something cool.

271
00:24:51,360 --> 00:24:59,040
And I got all this weird stuff like, you know, animation sales and I I I have a big pile of stuff still of all the things people send me.

272
00:24:59,280 --> 00:25:00,320
Yeah, I've never heard that term.

273
00:25:01,040 --> 00:25:02,240
That's kind of an interesting concept.

274
00:25:02,640 --> 00:25:04,240
It was kind of, it was like an honor system.

275
00:25:04,640 --> 00:25:06,960
It was freeware, you know, you didn't have to send me anything.

276
00:25:07,200 --> 00:25:12,480
But the idea was, you know, just if you feel like it, if you get some use out of this thing, send me something.

277
00:25:12,640 --> 00:25:15,840
Yeah., but there's something kind of cutesy.

278
00:25:16,240 --> 00:25:24,080
Like, I don't know, something about the iOS community I've noticed since coming over here is that there's just this kind of weird playfulness.

279
00:25:24,720 --> 00:25:25,920
And that feels like that.

280
00:25:26,080 --> 00:25:33,920
Now, I'm not saying that was an Apple thing necessarily then, but like, I'm trying to think like my mind is racing, like, could you have a concept like that today?

281
00:25:34,480 --> 00:25:35,520
I don't know how that would work.

282
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:37,680
I think, yeah, it's difficult today.

283
00:25:37,920 --> 00:25:47,040
I mean, I think it was a lot easier to sell software or people were, I don't want to use the word entitled, but you know kind of what I mean?

284
00:25:47,360 --> 00:25:51,360
It's like if you're trying to sell sell an app at two dollars, people complain at you.

285
00:25:51,680 --> 00:25:51,760
Yeah.

286
00:25:52,800 --> 00:26:05,280
But anyway, back then, somebody emailed me and they were working for a very large advertising firm and they said, you know, our accounting department doesn't understand the concept of cool.

287
00:26:05,920 --> 00:26:06,800
Can we send you money?

288
00:26:07,600 --> 00:26:09,040
I'm like, Sure.

289
00:26:10,880 --> 00:26:10,960
What?

290
00:26:11,920 --> 00:26:16,960
Like, this had never really occurred to me, for some reason, that you could just ask people for money.

291
00:26:17,920 --> 00:26:24,240
And so I timed it with the release of this new version, which had basically.

292
00:26:24,240 --> 00:26:27,920
Basically, unintentionally, all the old versions that were free died.

293
00:26:28,400 --> 00:26:30,080
And then I had the shareware version.

294
00:26:31,680 --> 00:26:39,760
So if you wanted to keep using the software, which had been around at this point for two years and lots of people were using it, you had to pay some money.

295
00:26:40,800 --> 00:26:43,920
And so it actually did extremely well.

296
00:26:44,240 --> 00:26:46,720
I was about to say, like, how was there like blowback?

297
00:26:47,280 --> 00:26:47,680
Not really.

298
00:26:48,400 --> 00:27:00,560
I mean, I think I'd built up a, I mean, that so this is one of the things about, I think, the iOS community and the Apple community generally is if you have goodwill, it's like the most.

299
00:27:01,360 --> 00:27:04,160
invaluable currency as a developer.

300
00:27:04,800 --> 00:27:10,800
And it's like I put the tip jar into Pcalc and people put money in it.

301
00:27:11,360 --> 00:27:12,720
You know, they don't have to.

302
00:27:12,880 --> 00:27:14,640
There's no reason for them to do so.

303
00:27:15,200 --> 00:27:18,960
But they do it because they kind of like what I do and want to support it.

304
00:27:19,360 --> 00:27:22,560
And, you know, here's a little extra thing.

305
00:27:22,960 --> 00:27:33,520
I mean, there's occasional people who complain, but yeah, that's definitely something I I've noticed to switch over because I used to live in the Android world and there's lots of things I liked about that too.

306
00:27:33,680 --> 00:27:36,640
But the concept of a tip jar was not.

307
00:27:36,880 --> 00:27:38,960
wasn't really something you saw anywhere.

308
00:27:39,360 --> 00:27:51,200
And along with that, you didn't have a bunch of apps that have these little, weird touches or like an About screen like Peacock, where it's like that it almost feels like that's the developer giving back.

309
00:27:51,360 --> 00:27:59,920
Like there's this give and take thing, maybe it's like Coolware, where it's like it's not necessarily just a transaction where I give you money, you give me product.

310
00:28:00,320 --> 00:28:02,080
There's this sort of relationship that exists.

311
00:28:02,640 --> 00:28:02,720
Yeah.

312
00:28:02,960 --> 00:28:05,440
And I mean, that's what I like to think of it.

313
00:28:05,680 --> 00:28:10,240
I mean, it's like, um, you know, Peacock has been around 27 years at this point.

314
00:28:10,400 --> 00:28:12,560
So there have been people using it for a long time.

315
00:28:13,040 --> 00:28:19,680
And, you know, there's people using it who weren't born when it came out, which is a disturbing thing to think about.

316
00:28:20,320 --> 00:28:32,400
But it's, yeah, there is a kind of, you try and like, you know, I'm not going to screw you over and, you know, I'm going to give you a nice product for a fair price.

317
00:28:33,120 --> 00:28:35,920
And people then kind of respect you for that.

318
00:28:36,480 --> 00:28:44,480
And, yeah, you can get you can get this kind of, you know, sort of symbiotic relationship, I guess.

319
00:28:44,720 --> 00:28:44,720
Yeah.

320
00:28:45,040 --> 00:28:47,360
No, I I definitely see that.

321
00:28:48,640 --> 00:28:52,880
It's like if you're not in the world, everything you're saying probably sounds really cheesy.

322
00:28:53,040 --> 00:28:57,280
It probably would have sounded cheesy to me a couple of years ago, but I know exactly what you're talking about.

323
00:28:57,440 --> 00:29:05,440
And it's it's like a weird, uh, like community relationship thing between the creators and the users.

324
00:29:06,160 --> 00:29:06,240
Yeah.

325
00:29:06,640 --> 00:29:18,560
And that's what it was like back in the early days as well, you know, because like with all of these things, you could just email the people who were writing the software and you could talk to them.

326
00:29:19,120 --> 00:29:25,760
And like when I was doing drag thing, which I don't think I've ever said what it was, but it, you know, it was a sort of app switch and launch a dock thing.

327
00:29:27,920 --> 00:29:34,880
There were like three or four other people who were working on fairly well regarded similar products and we would swap code.

328
00:29:35,440 --> 00:29:41,600
You know, if somebody worked out how to do something, it's like, oh, how do you display this particular icon or do this kind of thing?

329
00:29:42,080 --> 00:29:44,320
You know, we would share that information.

330
00:29:44,640 --> 00:29:48,560
It wasn't done in a kind of territorial way.

331
00:29:49,360 --> 00:29:54,560
So, you know, I knew all those folk and some of them worked at Apple as well, as it turned out.

332
00:29:55,440 --> 00:29:55,520
Yeah.

333
00:29:55,760 --> 00:29:57,840
So there was a nice community feel back then.

334
00:29:58,160 --> 00:29:59,840
And I think there is still a nice community.

335
00:30:01,120 --> 00:30:03,120
The difference is the scale of it these days.

336
00:30:03,440 --> 00:30:04,000
It's so big.

337
00:30:04,480 --> 00:30:04,560
Right.

338
00:30:05,040 --> 00:30:21,840
That there's probably, you know, hundreds of developers who, you know, highly skilled, putting out really good apps that I just don't know about because I haven't come across them and, you know, and I think it's difficult for people to kind of break into it.

339
00:30:22,640 --> 00:30:26,640
But that's the difference is there's just, you know, so, so much going on there.

340
00:30:27,040 --> 00:30:27,120
Yeah.

341
00:30:27,600 --> 00:30:34,880
I can say as someone who was not part of that at the beginning of this year or I guess last year, depending on when this episode comes out.

342
00:30:35,040 --> 00:30:39,200
And then now I feel like I've been sort of fully embraced by this community.

343
00:30:40,480 --> 00:30:41,920
It's like a night and day difference.

344
00:30:42,240 --> 00:30:51,040
And for me, it was during WWDC as a completely not in California bystander on Twitter.

345
00:30:52,080 --> 00:30:56,080
This was the first year I started like just kind of tweeting and replying to people.

346
00:30:56,720 --> 00:31:01,840
And it snowballed so quickly because everybody was just excited about all the same things.

347
00:31:02,560 --> 00:31:04,000
And I happened to be working on a thing.

348
00:31:04,160 --> 00:31:19,440
And people are just like extremely beneficial with their time and would be like, oh, you might, you know, here's, like you're saying, people would literally DM me code like, oh, this is a little helper I wrote to solve this problem or whatever.

349
00:31:19,840 --> 00:31:33,520
And I've been trying to do the same thing now, but it's it is really wild when you sort of somehow break into that fold of iOS developers on Twitter because there's all this like shared resource going on.

350
00:31:34,480 --> 00:31:34,560
Yeah.

351
00:31:34,800 --> 00:31:43,360
And like when the iPhone SDK first came out, there was a similar sort of, you know, frontier feel to it.

352
00:31:43,520 --> 00:31:46,560
And you have a lot of people sharing tips.

353
00:31:46,720 --> 00:32:01,920
And it's like, and I mean, these days., the absolute difference is there are Apple people on Twitter who are sharing things, you know, and you can find out, oh, this is the person who was up on stage at WWDC and I have their email address or I know who they are on Twitter.

354
00:32:03,040 --> 00:32:13,040
I can ask them a question and, you know, sometimes they're quite busy because they have a lot of things to do, but sometimes, you know, people will answer and say, oh, no, you should probably do this or whatever.

355
00:32:13,520 --> 00:32:13,600
Yeah.

356
00:32:13,840 --> 00:32:20,000
I had Ari, the leader on not work, workflow, but shortcuts now.

357
00:32:20,240 --> 00:32:20,320
Yeah.

358
00:32:20,640 --> 00:32:24,640
He like responded to a tweet once that I made and I was kind of sarcastic and I felt bad because.

359
00:32:24,640 --> 00:32:26,000
I was like, oh, I'm sorry.

360
00:32:26,080 --> 00:32:26,880
I know you work on this.

361
00:32:27,200 --> 00:32:32,000
And like, yeah, he's obviously really busy, but he took the time to sort of be like, oh, this should work.

362
00:32:32,160 --> 00:32:33,040
Oh, maybe that doesn't work.

363
00:32:33,200 --> 00:32:34,000
I'll go back to the team.

364
00:32:34,720 --> 00:32:36,480
And that was a wild interaction to have.

365
00:32:36,880 --> 00:32:36,960
Yeah.

366
00:32:37,280 --> 00:32:55,200
And, and, uh, it's been kind of like, I got into doing podcasts, you know, what feels like relatively recently for me, you know, it was probably 2014, which I guess is now five years at this point.

367
00:32:55,440 --> 00:32:55,520
Yeah.

368
00:32:56,000 --> 00:33:02,400
But, you know, that I think it was kind of the IOS stuff really kickstarted the community a bit.

369
00:33:02,960 --> 00:33:15,920
And, you know, since I got involved in doing the podcasting and was more of a visible face, you know, my sort of, I don't want to use the word stardom because that's exactly what you mean.

370
00:33:16,480 --> 00:33:23,520
That's a terrible, terrible thing because it's like, nobody knows who I am outside an extremely small bubble.

371
00:33:25,280 --> 00:33:39,040
But that kind of doing that podcasting stuff and trying to engage slightly more with people in the community and, you know, has really helped my sort of profile.

372
00:33:39,840 --> 00:33:48,480
And, you know, I'm now sort of, you know, in your intro you were saying, you know, nobody needs to be introduced to who I am.

373
00:33:48,720 --> 00:33:51,920
And I was listening to that thinking, God, that's terrifying.

374
00:33:52,800 --> 00:33:57,440
You know, and I kind of like, A, I never want to believe my own hype.

375
00:33:58,400 --> 00:34:02,720
Because, you know, the second you think, Oh yeah, I'm a big deal, you know, you've lost it.

376
00:34:03,040 --> 00:34:03,440
Oh yeah.

377
00:34:03,840 --> 00:34:12,160
But yeah, it's kind of been a weird ride over the last, like, even just five years because, you know, Peacock has done really well.

378
00:34:12,240 --> 00:34:34,720
Well, I think one of the sort of the inciting incidents in that was when the Peacock has a notification center widget and Apple very briefly banned it, saying I think this was in 2014 when widgets first appeared.

379
00:34:35,280 --> 00:34:40,720
Like I was being featured on the App Store for this widget as a great example of a notification center widget.

380
00:34:41,120 --> 00:34:48,800
And I was also being told that I had to remove it because it, you know, contravened various developer guidelines.

381
00:34:49,280 --> 00:34:53,280
Did they they pulled it from the store or they wouldn't let you submit new builds?

382
00:34:54,000 --> 00:34:57,600
They told me at the time I had like three weeks to remove the feature.

383
00:34:57,760 --> 00:34:58,320
Oh, yeah.

384
00:34:59,040 --> 00:35:02,080
Or they would pull the builds down or pull the app down.

385
00:35:03,200 --> 00:35:07,440
And you know how the Apple says, you know, running to the press never works.

386
00:35:08,400 --> 00:35:08,480
Yeah.

387
00:35:09,200 --> 00:35:09,840
That's not true.

388
00:35:10,320 --> 00:35:10,400
Yeah.

389
00:35:10,800 --> 00:35:17,840
I mean, it's something that one can only like, you should never run to the press as your first thing.

390
00:35:19,600 --> 00:35:20,560
You should try and talk to people.

391
00:35:20,720 --> 00:35:24,400
Well, it's like you were saying earlier, goodwill is capital.

392
00:35:24,480 --> 00:35:26,400
capital that you can build up and you can spend.

393
00:35:26,560 --> 00:35:26,640
Yeah.

394
00:35:26,960 --> 00:35:29,040
And running to the press is spending that goodwill.

395
00:35:29,200 --> 00:35:29,280
Yeah.

396
00:35:29,680 --> 00:35:33,760
This in this case with Apple particularly, but that seems like that's an important goodwill to have.

397
00:35:34,080 --> 00:35:34,160
Yeah.

398
00:35:34,320 --> 00:35:37,920
I mean, it's like the thing is like Apple is not like one person.

399
00:35:38,320 --> 00:35:38,560
Right.

400
00:35:38,800 --> 00:35:44,800
You know, so there's people in the engineering team who like you see what you've built and think it's amazing and they're really supportive.

401
00:35:45,040 --> 00:35:49,120
And then there's people in another team who might not want something to work the way it works.

402
00:35:49,280 --> 00:35:55,600
And, you know, so there's lots of different groups inside Apple and all have completely different opinions on things.

403
00:35:57,920 --> 00:36:00,400
But yeah, at the time I like I.

404
00:36:00,560 --> 00:36:10,720
I, you know, I kind of raised an appeal on it and that was shot down and I was told under no circumstances would this be changed.

405
00:36:11,760 --> 00:36:15,600
And so I, I, I didn't even like deliberately run to the press.

406
00:36:15,920 --> 00:36:25,040
I kind of tweeted about it and because I was, you know, I was pretty angry because I had done a lot of work to, to write this thing.

407
00:36:25,280 --> 00:36:32,720
And it was very tough because I think at that point there was like a 64 megabyte memory limit or something on, on widgets.

408
00:36:32,880 --> 00:36:36,480
And so I had done all this work to get it running in the, in the small.

409
00:36:38,400 --> 00:36:39,200
space that I had.

410
00:36:40,080 --> 00:36:45,840
And so I tweeted about it in a grumpy fashion and these tweets basically went viral.

411
00:36:46,080 --> 00:36:51,840
And I mean, I do know some people in the press and they were kind enough to retweet things.

412
00:36:52,160 --> 00:37:01,520
So, you know, it got coverage and then it got more coverage and then it got coverage to the point that it was, you know, like being mentioned in print newspapers or something.

413
00:37:01,920 --> 00:37:02,480
Holy cow.

414
00:37:04,000 --> 00:37:08,720
And I got a call like 48 hours later or something like that.

415
00:37:08,880 --> 00:37:10,160
I can't remember the exact timeline.

416
00:37:10,400 --> 00:37:13,840
It was pretty short, but they basically said., You're all good.

417
00:37:16,400 --> 00:37:25,920
And that was a that was a I mean, we did very well out of it because of all the publicity, you know, it sort of made Peacock more visible.

418
00:37:27,200 --> 00:37:28,960
But it wasn't worth it in terms of the stress.

419
00:37:29,600 --> 00:37:38,000
You know, a lot of these times you're thinking, you know, I'm going against the company that is, you know, in charge of my entire livelihood.

420
00:37:38,640 --> 00:37:38,800
Right.

421
00:37:40,560 --> 00:37:46,160
I don't want to piss them off, but I don't I don't seem to have any other option.

422
00:37:46,320 --> 00:37:47,520
Well, I mean, I had options.

423
00:37:47,600 --> 00:38:28,400
I could have just pulled it but i didn't think that was right because you know apple had done their own calculator notification center widget on the mac so i figured well if it's good enough for the mac it's clearly good enough for ios to have these kind of inter more interactive widgets which was what they had a problem with but yeah so that that incident uh kind of seemed to propel me somewhat more into the the spotlight and then there's been a number of things since then and i've tried to be tried to be a reasonable member of the community and help people and you always think you can do more, because I've got, to a certain extent, I've got a platform.

424
00:38:29,040 --> 00:38:32,880
So it's kind of like I should do something good with it.

425
00:38:33,440 --> 00:38:38,960
And with the widget stuff, it was like I kind of got my foot in the door and wedged open the door for other people.

426
00:38:39,600 --> 00:38:52,560
Well, this reminds me of over the summer, or right before iOS 13 came out, the developer of Allegory, that note taking app, he was having some problems with, was it with the Catalyst app?

427
00:38:52,880 --> 00:38:54,000
I don't remember the specifics.

428
00:38:54,160 --> 00:38:58,480
I just remember he got rejected for what seemed like a really bizarre reason.

429
00:38:58,720 --> 00:39:00,400
And he's a really good developer.

430
00:39:00,720 --> 00:39:02,720
and, you know, has a kind of a decent following.

431
00:39:02,960 --> 00:39:09,120
But I think you were the one that kind of elevated him up whenever you retweeted and was like, hey, this needs to be looked at.

432
00:39:09,200 --> 00:39:10,160
This is kind of a big deal.

433
00:39:10,560 --> 00:39:13,120
And that kind of blew up in a similar fashion to what you're talking about.

434
00:39:13,440 --> 00:39:13,520
Yeah.

435
00:39:13,680 --> 00:39:15,040
I mean, I think it's not just me.

436
00:39:15,200 --> 00:39:19,520
I mean, I think there are, like, the developer community tries to look after their own.

437
00:39:20,160 --> 00:39:20,400
I think.

438
00:39:21,360 --> 00:39:25,440
And, you know, people would do that for me and I would do that for other people.

439
00:39:25,840 --> 00:39:31,200
And especially when it's something like there's a kind of dubious app store decision or something.

440
00:39:31,840 --> 00:39:32,000
Right.

441
00:39:32,480 --> 00:39:36,560
That you know that that's probably just someone.

442
00:39:38,000 --> 00:39:44,800
who's being very strict with the rules and that's not really the intent of the rules or something like that.

443
00:39:45,040 --> 00:39:49,760
And you think, well, if this gets a bit more visibility, it'll get passed up the chain and stuff.

444
00:39:50,160 --> 00:39:54,800
But that's the other thing is you don't want to, it's the nuclear option.

445
00:39:55,200 --> 00:39:56,400
Yeah, yeah, exactly.

446
00:39:57,120 --> 00:40:03,520
I'm curious, like, because I'm sure, you know, ostensibly this podcast is about, uh, what it's like launching something.

