00:41:11,440 --> 00:41:33,920
So that happened because there was an international meet and greet at WWDC and Phil was there and he was, you know, sort of working the room of developers and I was talking to him and I told him what I did and I gave him a business card and I said, you know, if you want a free copy of my app, let me know and I'll give you one.

458
00:41:34,640 --> 00:41:42,320
And he got back to me and he, or I think it was his assistant got back to me and said, you know, can Phil have a serial number?

459
00:41:42,480 --> 00:41:43,680
And I'm like, absolutely.

460
00:41:45,280 --> 00:41:56,240
And some time after that I got an email saying, you know, we'd like to bundle this with a upcoming machine and, you know, for a certain royalty.

461
00:41:56,400 --> 00:41:59,680
And the royalty wasn't huge, but it was a large number of copies.

462
00:41:59,920 --> 00:42:00,880
So, you know, we did get.

463
00:42:01,360 --> 00:42:04,080
I didn't know they ever really bundled third party apps at all.

464
00:42:04,320 --> 00:42:10,480
Yeah, back in the early days, especially the early days of macOS 10 when there weren't quite as many apps around.

465
00:42:11,040 --> 00:42:11,200
Right.

466
00:42:13,760 --> 00:42:20,400
The system calculator was a very, very basic one, which resembled the one from the kind of the early classic Mac days.

467
00:42:22,080 --> 00:42:26,400
So there was, you know, they would actually do it territory specific as well.

468
00:42:26,640 --> 00:42:29,760
You know, there was a different calculator got bundled in France and things.

469
00:42:30,800 --> 00:42:33,840
I think because they had a French localization and we didn't.

470
00:42:34,000 --> 00:42:34,240
Right.

471
00:42:34,560 --> 00:42:35,200
Or something like that.

472
00:42:35,600 --> 00:42:36,640
But it, you know, there was a.

473
00:42:36,640 --> 00:42:46,080
there was this kind of deals and eventually, you know, the deals went away and Apple wrote their own stuff, but it was still nice to have, you know, a year of royalties for that.

474
00:42:46,400 --> 00:42:49,440
And it was a not a small number.

475
00:42:50,640 --> 00:42:55,360
So, you know, I feel very fondly towards Phil.

476
00:42:55,920 --> 00:42:57,520
I don't know what he feels towards me.

477
00:42:59,520 --> 00:43:07,520
But I mean, I've had things like there was another podcast I was on, you know, debug Rennie Ritchie and Guy English.

478
00:43:08,000 --> 00:43:08,720
Okay, I know them.

479
00:43:08,960 --> 00:43:10,000
I don't know the podcast though.

480
00:43:10,400 --> 00:43:19,600
Well, the podcast stopped some years back, but it was a it was a similar kind of interviewing developers in a in a conversational style, much like this.

481
00:43:21,600 --> 00:43:41,040
And I told a variety of stories on that and, you know, some that I possibly shouldn't have and the particular story was so the other backstory is that I later in Apple went on to work on the macOS 10 dock.

482
00:43:41,280 --> 00:43:44,160
That was my I spent eighteen months on that.

483
00:43:44,720 --> 00:43:46,640
Was that because of drag thing?

484
00:43:46,800 --> 00:43:47,520
Because like, yes.

485
00:43:47,680 --> 00:43:48,720
There's obviously connections.

486
00:43:49,280 --> 00:43:55,200
and pre the dock in macOS 10, there wasn't a concept of a dock like that, right?

487
00:43:55,600 --> 00:43:58,480
Yeah, I mean, it was a combination of things.

488
00:43:58,720 --> 00:44:09,920
It was like Next had a dock and most of the engineering management came from Next at that time, so, you know, they wanted a dock for the Mac as well.

489
00:44:11,520 --> 00:44:19,120
But, you know, DragThing was mentioned as one of the things in the design document as a possible inspiration.

490
00:44:19,680 --> 00:44:21,360
So, you know, they knew who I was.

491
00:44:22,000 --> 00:44:22,160
Right.

492
00:44:22,480 --> 00:44:24,800
But, so I worked away on that for eighteen months.

493
00:44:25,440 --> 00:44:38,800
and I was working on it from Cork and it was a super secret project and Steve Jobs found out and he was extremely unhappy that a super secret feature was being worked on outside Cupertino.

494
00:44:39,280 --> 00:44:39,360
Oh.

495
00:44:40,480 --> 00:44:57,280
And so the famous quote from him which I use in pretty much every talk I've given is him talking to my boss's boss's boss with the line and I'm sorry if you're going to have to bleep this.

496
00:44:58,560 --> 00:45:03,120
It has come to my attention that the engineer working on the dock is in Ireland.

497
00:45:05,440 --> 00:45:13,440
So that was the start of, uh, I was told to move to Cupertino or else and I said no.

498
00:45:14,000 --> 00:45:20,720
Uh, and this went on for a while and eventually I got a second ultimatum to move over and I said no.

499
00:45:20,960 --> 00:45:29,200
And I was told, well, if you don't move over, uh, we're going to take you off the dock and the Finder and you're never going to get any more interesting work over again.

500
00:45:30,320 --> 00:45:31,760
And I resigned.

501
00:45:33,920 --> 00:45:37,520
Because that just felt like blackmail to me at the time.

502
00:45:37,680 --> 00:45:39,040
And I didn't want to live in the States.

503
00:45:39,280 --> 00:45:40,560
There was a reason why I was in Europe.

504
00:45:41,760 --> 00:45:44,720
So I I told that story.

505
00:45:45,120 --> 00:45:51,200
Oh, the final part of the story is like a few weeks after I resigned, they fired everyone else in Cork.

506
00:45:51,600 --> 00:45:52,320
So Oh man.

507
00:45:53,360 --> 00:45:58,240
They were basically planning to shut down the team and they wanted me to move to Cork.

508
00:45:58,880 --> 00:45:59,360
Okay, yeah.

509
00:45:59,520 --> 00:45:59,920
California.

510
00:46:00,640 --> 00:46:02,960
But they wouldn't tell me that they were planning to shut down the team.

511
00:46:04,240 --> 00:46:09,040
So they just gave me an ultimatum thinking that my reaction to that would be say, Yes please.

512
00:46:11,280 --> 00:46:12,720
And I didn't.

513
00:46:13,440 --> 00:46:16,480
So if I had stayed an extra few weeks, I would have got some severance money.

514
00:46:16,640 --> 00:46:16,800
Yeah.

515
00:46:18,720 --> 00:46:33,200
So I told this story and at a WWDC like three, four years ago, I was talking to Craig Federici, um, because I had seen him in a session or whatever, and I thought, well, I'll just corner him.

516
00:46:33,360 --> 00:46:38,640
And at the time, I made up these little pin badges of the Peacock app icon.

517
00:46:38,880 --> 00:46:38,960
Yeah.

518
00:46:39,360 --> 00:46:43,120
And I was giving them out to, to folk, uh, at the conference.

519
00:46:43,280 --> 00:46:44,880
And I thought, well, I want to give him one.

520
00:46:45,520 --> 00:46:48,800
And I went up to him and I sort of introduced myself.

521
00:46:49,280 --> 00:46:53,040
and, you know, kind of explain who I was.

522
00:46:53,680 --> 00:46:55,760
And I started to tell some story.

523
00:46:56,160 --> 00:46:59,520
And he said, Oh, yes, I've heard you tell that story on a podcast.

524
00:46:59,920 --> 00:47:00,240
Oh man.

525
00:47:01,120 --> 00:47:06,960
And I was like that sort of fear of, Well, there's only one podcast that can be.

526
00:47:08,720 --> 00:47:15,600
And I'm pretty sure I told things on that that, you know, weren't one hundred percent favorable to Apple.

527
00:47:16,960 --> 00:47:18,240
But, you know, he was really nice.

528
00:47:18,560 --> 00:47:24,800
And I don't know, it's just that sort of, you know, heart sync moment of like, this guy knows who I am.

529
00:47:25,520 --> 00:47:27,760
This is great, but it's also terrifying.

530
00:47:29,200 --> 00:47:37,520
It is really funny how we think of, like, there's this huge Apple community and podcasts and blogs and all this stuff.

531
00:47:37,920 --> 00:47:40,800
And that feels so apart from Apple, the black box.

532
00:47:40,960 --> 00:47:47,600
And it's like, we can't see in, but I always forget that they can see out and they probably consume all the same stuff that we all are.

533
00:47:48,080 --> 00:47:48,160
Yeah.

534
00:47:48,400 --> 00:47:52,800
And I've heard people say literally that, that they listen to all the same podcasts we do.

535
00:47:53,200 --> 00:47:53,840
They know all the things.

536
00:47:54,080 --> 00:47:56,960
They just can't chime in and comment on stuff.

537
00:47:57,520 --> 00:48:00,880
And, you know, I mean, you've seen things like, um, I've seen.

538
00:48:01,680 --> 00:48:08,400
I think it was connected that was featured in one of the keynote and I think ATP got called out on something else.

539
00:48:08,560 --> 00:48:11,120
You know, it's that kind of they know who we are.

540
00:48:11,680 --> 00:48:23,280
I mean, it's that that's again, it's kind of a little worrying, but I mean, you you talk to people like, you know, sometimes people will reach out.

541
00:48:23,680 --> 00:48:32,560
If you like, if I'm really frustrated and you badmouth some technology on Twitter because, you know, you've been banging your head against some API and it doesn't work.

542
00:48:32,720 --> 00:48:40,560
And, you know, the thing you really shouldn't do is sort of say, ah, the people who wrote this are a bunch of bozos or whatever.

543
00:48:40,880 --> 00:48:41,040
Right.

544
00:48:41,360 --> 00:48:45,680
Because they might follow you on Twitter or, you know, someone might point out the thing to them.

545
00:48:45,840 --> 00:48:51,440
And it's like, if I was in that situation, I wouldn't want people saying, you know, the stuff that I work on is bad.

546
00:48:51,760 --> 00:49:04,480
And I've had that happen a few times and, and, you know, kind of it drills into you that you shouldn't, you know, there are actual people who write all this stuff and they have the same feelings and things that we do.

547
00:49:04,880 --> 00:49:07,760
It like humanizes them whenever you actually hear from the person.

548
00:49:08,160 --> 00:49:08,240
Yeah.

549
00:49:08,560 --> 00:49:16,160
And, and, you know, sometimes, like, you'll know someone before they go over to Apple., so you might have some kind of rapport with them.

550
00:49:16,560 --> 00:49:20,720
And people in Apple won't leak stuff.

551
00:49:21,440 --> 00:49:23,120
Well, they don't leak it to me, generally.

552
00:49:25,120 --> 00:49:30,960
But people might reach out and say, you should probably do this rather than do this.

553
00:49:32,320 --> 00:49:36,320
Or if I say, I'm having this, I've run into this really bad problem.

554
00:49:37,120 --> 00:49:39,040
Here's a bug number, and I'll post that to Twitter.

555
00:49:39,440 --> 00:49:45,600
Sometimes people DM you and say, Yeah, we're looking at that now, and we'll let you know, kind of thing.

556
00:49:46,400 --> 00:49:48,320
So, as you say, it's this capital.

557
00:49:48,480 --> 00:50:27,120
capital and that's what I think is the sort of key thing in in our little community is trying to sort of you know spend it wisely and and get the kind of I don't know it's a kind of it's a weird thing you know thinking of it in sort of this kind of financial terms but it but it but it's like a you know it's like an energy meter in some RPG you know you've got to build up this energy and and you know use it when it's when it's wise to do so and the way you can build it up is by being a good member of the community and kind of trying to help people as you can.

558
00:50:27,520 --> 00:50:32,880
And, you know, sometimes you get people who kind of want more help than you can give them.

559
00:50:33,440 --> 00:50:46,080
And, you know, you should try and in a nice way say, you know, this documentation really helped me, you know, this sample code is good thing to look at, that sort of thing.

560
00:50:46,800 --> 00:50:49,680
But yeah, it's a it's a nice place.

561
00:50:50,960 --> 00:51:12,400
And, you know, the development has changed enormously in the from, you know, when I'm sending software around on floppy disks and uploading it to FTP servers to now when you have, you know, app stores and, you know, everything has changed in terms of the commercial side of things.

562
00:51:12,720 --> 00:51:19,600
It's really hard to sell apps now and it felt relatively easy to do so 25 years ago.

563
00:51:20,000 --> 00:51:24,160
You were certainly competing against less, but there was also a smaller market, right?

564
00:51:24,800 --> 00:51:24,880
Yeah.

565
00:51:25,120 --> 00:51:29,040
I mean, yeah, there's, but it's the kind of big fish, small pond thing.

566
00:51:29,520 --> 00:51:29,600
Yeah.

567
00:51:30,080 --> 00:51:36,480
You know, you can be the, you know, dock app or the calculator app because there's only like about ten.

568
00:51:37,200 --> 00:51:37,440
of them.

569
00:51:37,600 --> 00:51:37,680
Yeah.

570
00:51:38,480 --> 00:51:39,520
Yeah, that's not the case now.

571
00:51:39,680 --> 00:51:44,000
Unless you come up with something truly original, but that's And there's nothing truly original.

572
00:51:44,320 --> 00:51:44,400
Yeah.

573
00:51:44,640 --> 00:51:56,960
I mean, it was like I remember, I can't remember, it was like very early days of the App Store and like someone had an ukulele and I'm like, I wonder if there's an ukulele tuner app.

574
00:51:57,200 --> 00:51:58,800
And, you know, there's like ten of them.

575
00:52:00,000 --> 00:52:03,360
And it's like, so it's hard to find a niche.

576
00:52:04,720 --> 00:52:11,680
But on the other hand, you know, you can make something that's good and do it well.

577
00:52:11,920 --> 00:52:22,640
I mean, it doesn't guarantee success, but, um, and I think a lot of my success has to do with luck and being in the right place at the right time and being around for a long time.

578
00:52:23,040 --> 00:52:26,240
You know, there's nothing that helps success like prior success.

579
00:52:26,800 --> 00:52:38,400
And I think, I think there's that, uh, goodwill capital that we keep talking about, like, you're a member of the community that's contributed a lot, or at least I can say this from my perspective, having literally never talked to you before today.

580
00:52:39,120 --> 00:52:46,320
My impression of you has always been that you're a super nice developer who's been really like active in the community and helpful.

581
00:52:46,880 --> 00:52:52,880
And so whenever you put something out like dice which we should definitely talk about really quick before we run out of time here.

